                <pb facs="1172" n="1162" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg1162"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.3516/4–2546: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-04-25T10:00:00+03:00">April 25, 1946—10
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 25—9:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1325. ReDeptel 688, April 12.<anchor n="18" xml:id="d896-f1"/> Benninghoff
                    apparently unable to telegraph Dept from Dairen. He sent following telegram to
                    Soviet FonOff which FonOff repeated to us in its note 650, April 21: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“To Deputy Minister Lozovsky. American Embassy No. 17. Arrived April 7.
                            Cordially met by local authorities. Am doing everything necessary for
                            speedy opening. Please discuss with Soviet authorities question of
                            telegraphic communications between Embassy and Consulates in China, also
                            Washington. Inform Washington. Benninghoff.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
